{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## IMPLEMENTED — Gerrit 1337. Read-back verification, plus TWO STALE DOUBLES the held-out cells exposed.\n\n`set_parent`'s sub-task branch now re-reads the issue after writing and raises `NotImplementedError` when the parent did not move, naming both the requested and the still-observed parent. All 9 oracle cells pass, including the 7 never shown to the implementer. Gates: ruff clean, mypy 429, hygiene 0, raw-git-write 0, **2155 reconciler tests pass**, `_hierarchy.py` 199/800.\n\n### THE FINDING WORTH CARRYING: two committed doubles were indistinguishable from the platform bug\n\nThe implementer reported an honest conflict — a strict read-back broke `test_dc_epic_link_parent_39c1.py::test_subtask_parent_still_uses_fields_parent`, whose `_FakeClient` records a parent write but never reflects it. **That double's post-write payload is byte-identical to a DC instance exhibiting this exact bug.** It resolved it in PRODUCTION code, by treating a read-back that does not carry `parent` as 'no evidence' and returning.\n\n**I did not accept that, and the reasoning is the point.** It is defensible in the abstract — a fields-limited projection and a parentless issue are the same absence — but not at THIS call site: the read-back fetches the whole issue with no `fields=` projection, so for a sub-task the key is present whenever a parent is. The branch was unreachable on real DC and existed only to satisfy a double that does not model state. That is production code shaped by a test's limitation, and it reintroduces the silent pass this ticket exists to remove — the same 'we could not check must not read as success' rule I had just written into 3fe5's provisioning assertion hours earlier.\n\nSo instead: made the doubles faithful, and made the check strict. **A second stale double surfaced immediately** — `test_dc_live_oracles_discriminate_5200.py::_FakeIssue`, same shape, same problem — which the first fix would have masked entirely.\n\nBoth now apply the parent write into their `.raw`, so their cells keep asserting the write SHAPE (their actual subject) rather than failing on persistence. Added two cells: a SET whose read-back shows no parent MUST raise, and its mirror — a CLEAR is satisfied by an absent parent, so 'absent means no parent' is not misread as 'absent always fails'.\n\n**This is session-log finding #2 recurring** (a change makes production call a NEW method on an injected collaborator → every existing double is a candidate vacuous oracle). Here the new call was a second `issue()` read, and TWO doubles were silently wrong. Neither failed to tell me — they failed loudly only because the production check was strict.\n\n### AC status\n\n- [x] AC1 live behaviour established (capability map: 204-and-ignores)\n- [x] AC2 dead — DC does not error\n- [x] AC3 `set_parent` verifies by read-back rather than silently claiming success\n- [ ] AC4 a live cell proves it — needs a harness run CONTAINING 1337; run 30867072561 does not."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785807676840200001,"uuid":"a6d54ad4-d1eb-4933-93e4-d635a5748e7d"}